{{define "body"}}
<div class="picture">
  <h2>grid — {{.View.Cols}} × {{.View.Rows}} cells at ({{.View.Q}}, {{.View.R}}), 1:{{.View.Stride}}, turn {{.View.Turn}}</h2>
  {{if .Warning}}<p class="warning">{{.Warning}}</p>{{end}}
  <img src="{{.ImageURL}}" alt="the {{.View.Layer}} layer at one pixel per hex">
  <p class="doc">One pixel per hex, or {{.View.Scale}}, so a million tiles can be looked at in one image.
  What it distorts: a hex row's centers are √3·r apart and a column's are 1.5·r, so drawing both as one
  pixel stretches this picture vertically by about fifteen percent and flattens the half-hex column stagger.
  That is the price of the view.</p>
  <h2>sampling</h2>
  <div class="row">{{range .Strides}}<a href="{{.URL}}"{{if .Current}} class="current"{{end}}>{{.Label}}</a>{{end}}</div>
  <p class="doc">One cell per <em>n</em> hexes. The world is 65,535 hexes across, so a wide window at a
  coarse sampling is how all of it fits in one image.</p>
  <p class="doc">A pixel here spans about 6·<em>n</em> miles, so a feature gets
  <strong>wavelength ÷ 6<em>n</em></strong> pixels. The continental scale is 6,000 miles: 83 pixels
  across at 1:12, 32 at 1:31, 11 at 1:88. Below a few tens of pixels a feature stops being a shape and
  becomes a dot, and a wide window full of dots is a wide window, not a broken render.</p>
  <p class="doc">Aliasing is a second, smaller effect. A stride point-samples rather than averages, so
  the shortest wavelength it can carry is <strong>12·<em>n</em> miles</strong> and octaves below that
  arrive aliased — the Nyquist rule of the octave ladder with the sampling grid changed. At 1:88 the
  limit is 1,056 miles and the continental ladder's two finest octaves, at 750 and 375, are both under
  it. Measured, that adds about half again to the pixel-to-pixel variation: it roughens the edges of
  features, it does not create them.</p>
  <p class="doc">So if a whole-world window looks like gravel, read it as a statement about the
  configuration before reading it as one about the renderer. The world is 393,210 miles across and
  <code>continental_wavelength_miles</code> is 6,000, which is sixty-five continents from edge to edge
  however large the image is. The field to move is that one.</p>
</div>
<div class="panel">{{template "controls" .}}</div>
{{end}}
{{template "layout" .}}
